<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A checkout test needs an Android device, a database and a live server before it can check one price calculation. The calculation is small; constructing its dependencies has pulled the rest of the application into the test.</p>

Follow the dependency a caller must understand, then ask what change would cross that boundary. File count and interface count are clues, not a score for architectural quality.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: visual -->
### Separate constructing a dependency from using its contract

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph Wiring["Application composition boundary"]
      Factory["Choose and construct dependencies"]
      Adapter["Repository implementation"]
      Transport["HTTP client and local storage"]
      Adapter -->|"uses implementation details"| Transport
      Factory -->|"constructs"| Adapter
    end
    subgraph Feature["Checkout feature"]
      VM["Checkout state owner"]
      Contract["Cart data contract"]
      VM -->|"load cart; receive application values"| Contract
    end
    Factory -->|"supplies dependency"| VM
    Adapter -.->|"implements"| Contract
```

The solid arrows name construction or use; the dotted arrow names implementation of the contract. The feature still depends on what the contract promises: values, failure behavior and lifetime. The wiring chooses the implementation. An injected concrete class can be enough when that coupling is intentional; add an interface when substitution or hiding implementation details serves the caller.

[Source: Android's dependency-injection guide](https://developer.android.com/training/dependency-injection).

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
### Review a concrete change across the boundary

Try “replace the HTTP client” and “change checkout's discount rule.” The first should stay behind the data contract if callers are meant to be transport-independent. The second belongs with checkout policy; hiding it behind a generic utility name does not make it cohesive.

A transport-specific interface can be useful without being a domain abstraction. Retrofit's annotations declare an HTTP service contract. Add a separate application-facing contract only where callers should be insulated from those HTTP details. Keep the boundary's errors, cancellation and ownership explicit too; an interface name alone does not hide them.

[Source: Retrofit's service declaration model](https://square.github.io/retrofit/).
